<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galbert of Bruges</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died c. 1134</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galbert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> was a cleric and notary in the service of the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> account of the murder of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-good">Charles the Good</a> in 1127 and the succession crisis that followed, written almost day by day as events unfolded, is one of the most detailed contemporary narratives of the twelfth century. It shows the Flemish towns claiming a voice in choosing the count before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thierry-of-alsace">Thierry of Alsace</a> prevailed in 1128.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galbert-of-bruges.jpg" alt="Galbert of Bruges" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KareldeGoede.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
